# Tests come first and carry their id

Specification §10.1: the gates "run on every pull request, they block merge, and they are written
before the code they protect."

```ts
/** @spec BR-02 — one chair, overlapping range, concurrent attempts. */
test("N simultaneous bookings on one chair produce exactly one appointment", async () => { ... });
```

`scripts/trace.py` reads the `@spec` tag. An untagged test covers nothing as far as the build is
concerned.

## Gate tests run against real PostgreSQL

Four gates, defined in §10.1 and built in M0 before any business endpoint exists:

| Gate | Asserts |
| --- | --- |
| Tenant isolation | Two tenants interleaved over a pool of size 1 never see each other's rows; an unset context reads zero rows |
| BR-02 concurrency | N simultaneous bookings on one chair produce exactly one appointment |
| Vault non-recoverability | No plaintext from a full dump plus every server-held secret, without the PIN |
| Layout integrity | `python3 prototype/check-layout.py` |

**A mocked database in a gate test is worse than no gate, because it reports green.** The spec puts
it directly: "An isolation test that does not execute `CREATE POLICY` is testing the test." Spin up
a container, run the real migrations, apply the real policies.

The isolation gate must set pool `max: 1`, forcing every request onto one physical connection, and
interleave two tenants. That is the only configuration that reproduces the R12 leak.

## Discipline

- Reproduce a bug with a failing test before fixing it.
- Never weaken, skip or delete a gate to make a build pass. A red gate is information.
- Assert the property, not the implementation. "No cross-tenant row is returned" survives a
  refactor; "calls `withTenant` twice" does not.
- Milestone exit criteria in §10 are acceptance tests with fixed names — see
  `docs/plan/BUILD-PROTOCOL.md`.
